<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dun Nechtain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/685">685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Nechtansmere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dun Nechtain, or Nechtansmere, was fought on 20 May 685 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-beli">Bridei son of Beli</a> and the Northumbrians under King Ecgfrith, who was killed. The Pictish victory destroyed the Northumbrian army and ended English domination over the Picts and much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain.jpg" alt="Battle of Dun Nechtain" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greenshed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictish_Stone_at_Aberlemno_Church_Yard_-_Battle_Scene_Detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the 7th century the Northumbrians extended their territory northward, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s evidence points to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> being subjugated under King Oswald and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a>. Soon after Ecgfrith succeeded Oswiu in 670, the Picts rebelled and were crushed at the Battle of Two Rivers; the king of Fortriu, Drest mac Donuel, was deposed and replaced by Bridei. By 679 the Northumbrian hegemony seems to have begun to fall apart, with a Mercian victory over Ecgfrith in which his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfwine-of-deira">Ælfwine of Deira</a> was killed.</p><p>Sieges at Dunnottar in 680 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundurn">Dundurn</a> in 682, most probably by Bridei's forces, threatened Ecgfrith's hold on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. Bridei, who was closely involved with the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, is also unlikely to have welcomed the new Northumbrian-sponsored see at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abercorn">Abercorn</a>, whose bishop Trumwine was consecrated Bishop of the Picts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ecgfrith attacked Fortriu against the counsel of his advisers, among them Cuthbert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>; the consensus is that he meant to reassert control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. Bede gives the fullest account, though a brief one, with further detail in the annals of Ulster and Tigernach and in Nennius's Historia Brittonum. The Picts feigned retreat and drew the Northumbrians into an ambush at a lake in the mountains, where their army was defeated.</p><p>The site is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says only that the battle took place in straits of inaccessible mountains, Nennius calls it Gueith Linn Garan, the Battle of Crane Lake, and the Irish annals name Dún Nechtain, Nechtan's Fort. Since George Chalmers in the early 19th century it has been placed at Dunnichen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a>, but in 2006 the historian Alex Woolf pointed to the absence of inaccessible mountains in mid-Angus and argued for Dunachton, on Loch Insh in Badenoch, a name of the same origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> recounts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> recovered the lands held by the Northumbrians and the Dál Riatan Scots, and that the Northumbrians who did not flee were killed or enslaved. The diocese of the Picts was abandoned, Trumwine and his monks fleeing to Whitby, which stalled Roman Catholic expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Battles between Northumbrians and Picts went on, as in 697, when Berhtred son of Beornhæth was killed, but Pictish independence from Northumbria was permanently secured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dun Nechtain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 684 his army raided Brega in Ireland, and in 685 he led an invasion of Pictland that ended in disaster at the <b>Battle of Nechtansmere</b>, where the Pictish king Bridei son of Beli killed him and destroyed his army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their greatest victory came on 20 May 685, when Bridei son of Beli defeated and killed the Northumbrian king Ecgfrith at <b>Dun Nechtain</b>, often called Nechtansmere, ending Northumbrian domination north of the Forth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…were probably tributary to it until the reign of Bridei mac Beli, who defeated the Anglians at the <b>Battle of Dun Nechtain</b> in 685. Óengus mac Fergusa (reigned 729–761) held Dál Riata subject, and Caustantín mac Fergusa (793–820) placed his son Domnall on its throne, though Pictish attempts…</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Bridei son of Beli destroyed the Northumbrian army at <b>Dun Nechtain</b> in 685, ending English dominance in the north. Óengus son of Fergus, from 729 to 761, defeated rivals and even campaigned against Dál Riata and Strathclyde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Kingdom of Fortriu</a></li></ul></section>
